\begin{cases} y - z = -10, \\ z - x = 3, \\ x - y = 7. \end{cases}
\] Add all three equations: \((y - z) + (z - x) + (x - y) = -10 + 3 + 7 \Rightarrow 0 = 0\). This is always true, so the system is consistent and has infinitely many solutions. To find a particular solution, express \(x, y, z\) in terms of a parameter \(t\). From the second equation, we have \(z = x + 3\). Substitute into the first equation: \(y - (x + 3) = -10 \Rightarrow y = x - 7\). So, we can write \(\mathbf{v} = \begin{pmatrix} x \\ x - 7 \\ x + 3 \end{pmatrix} = x \begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ 1 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix} - 7 \begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ 0 \\ 0 \end{pmatrix} + 3 \begin{pmatrix} 0 \\ 0 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix}\). The third equation \(x - y = 7\) is automatically satisfied by this form. Finally, use the condition \(\mathbf{v} \cdot \mathbf{a} = 0\): \[\mathbf{v} \cdot \mathbf{a} = x \cdot 2 + (x - 7) \cdot 0 + (x + 3) \cdot 1 = 2x + x + 3 = 3x + 3 = 0 \Rightarrow x = -1. \] So, \(\mathbf{v} = \begin{pmatrix} -1 \\ -8 \\ 2 \end{pmatrix}.\)</answer>

